A. SOLID Principles
S – Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

Ek class ki ek primary responsibility honi chahiye.

Tumhare code mein:
- TextElement text handle karta hai.
- ImageElement image path handle karta hai.
- Document elements manage karta hai.
- FileStorage file saving karta hai.
Agar file saving mein error aaye toh TextElement ko change nahi karna padega.

O – Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

Software extension ke liye open aur existing code modification ke liye closed hona chahiye.

Example:

Kal tumhe VideoElement add karna hai.

Tum nayi class bana sakti ho:
class VideoElement implements DocumentElement {
    public String render() {
        return "[Video]";
    }
}


Document class ko change karne ki zarurat nahi hai.

L – Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

Child class ko parent/interface ki jagah use kar paana chahiye bina expected behaviour break kiye.

Example:
DocumentElement element = new TextElement("Hello");


Ya:
DocumentElement element = new ImageElement("photo.jpg");


Dono ko DocumentElement ke roop mein use kar sakte hain.

I – Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

Kisi class ko aise methods implement karne ke liye force nahi karna chahiye jinki usko zarurat nahi hai.

Example:

TextElement ko sirf render() implement karna hai. Usko saveToDatabase(), printImage() jaise unrelated methods implement nahi karne padte.

D – Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

High-level classes ko concrete implementations ke bajaye abstractions par depend karna chahiye.

Tumhare code mein:
private Persistence storage;


Na ki:
private FileStorage storage;


Isliye FileStorage aur DBStorage ke beech easily switch kar sakte hain.